# =============================================================================
# Hisab · Apache / LiteSpeed configuration
#
# This file is deployed to the document root (public_html on Hostinger).
#
# It does four jobs, in order of how much damage getting them wrong does:
#
#   1. Forces HTTPS. THE VAULT DOES NOT WORK WITHOUT IT — crypto.subtle is
#      undefined outside a secure context, so on plain http the vault screen
#      fails while every other page works, which is a confusing way to find out.
#   2. Blocks the files that must never be served: documentation, the Python
#      tools, the Laravel application layer, dotfiles.
#   3. Sets the MIME types Hostinger does not send correctly on its own.
#   4. Sets security headers, including a real Content-Security-Policy.
#
# After editing, run:  python tools/check-pages.py
# It verifies the CSP hash below still matches the inline theme block in the
# pages. If they disagree the browser silently blocks that script and every
# page paints one white frame before correcting itself.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0 · No directory listings, ever
#
# Without this, a URL ending in a directory with no index file returns a
# browsable index of it — which on this app means a listing of every module.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Options -Indexes

DirectoryIndex index.html index.php

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0.1 · This directory sits under another site's document root
#
# hisab.gulfrabit.com's root is /domains/gulfrabit.com/public_html/hisab, so
# gulfrabit.com's own .htaccess is a PARENT of this one and its directives merge
# into this directory.
#
# Header and FilesMatch directives from a parent are additive, which is
# harmless here. mod_rewrite rules are the exception and are the reason this
# file re-declares `RewriteEngine On` in every block: rewrite rules do not
# inherit into a child directory unless the parent explicitly says
# `RewriteOptions Inherit`, and re-enabling the engine here means this
# directory's rules stand on their own.
#
# If GulfRabit's own .htaccess ever grows a catch-all front-controller rewrite
# AND turns on inheritance, requests to this app would be swallowed by it. The
# symptom would be GulfRabit's pages appearing on the hisab subdomain. The fix
# is to add `RewriteOptions None` at the top of the block below.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 · One canonical address, over HTTPS
#
# CANONICAL_HOST is the only hostname this app answers on. Everything else
# redirects to it.
#
# This matters more than it looks, because of where Hostinger puts a subdomain.
# hisab.gulfrabit.com's document root is
#
#     /domains/gulfrabit.com/public_html/hisab
#
# which is INSIDE gulfrabit.com's own web root. So the identical files are also
# reachable at https://gulfrabit.com/hisab/ — a second entrance to a private
# app, where:
#
#   · root-absolute paths resolve against gulfrabit.com and 404 (this is what
#     404.html and the manifest scope depend on)
#   · localStorage is keyed to a different origin, so the ledger entered on one
#     address is invisible on the other and looks like data loss
#   · the vault's encrypted blob lives under whichever origin created it
#
# The rule below sends any other host to the canonical one, preserving the path.
# Inside this directory's .htaccess the rewrite pattern is already relative to
# the directory, so a request for gulfrabit.com/hisab/modules/ledger/list.html
# matches as "modules/ledger/list.html" and lands correctly on the subdomain.
#
# CHANGE BOTH LINES TOGETHER if the app ever moves to another address.
#
# The HTTPS test uses two conditions rather than one: %{HTTPS} is the direct
# signal and is what LiteSpeed sets on Hostinger, while X-Forwarded-Proto is
# what arrives through a proxy or CDN — where %{HTTPS} reads as off on an
# already-secure request and a single-condition rule redirects forever.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # Not the canonical host (this also catches gulfrabit.com/hisab/…).
  # The optional port group is not decoration: some proxies present HTTP_HOST
  # as "host:443", which fails a bare anchored match and redirects the correct
  # address to itself, forever.
  RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !^hisab\.gulfrabit\.com(:[0-9]+)?$ [NC]
  RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://hisab.gulfrabit.com/$1 [L,R=301,QSA]

  # Right host, wrong scheme
  RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
  RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !https
  RewriteRule ^ https://hisab.gulfrabit.com%{REQUEST_URI} [L,R=301]
</IfModule>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2 · What must never be served
#
# The frontend and the backend live in one repository, which is what makes a
# module a deletable vertical slice — and it means the web root contains files
# that are not for the web. Each pattern below is a file that would otherwise be
# readable by anyone who guessed its name.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Documentation and notes. endpoints.md describes every route, SECURITY.md
# describes the vault's key hierarchy, and context.md is a decision log. None of
# it is secret exactly, and none of it is anyone else's business.
<FilesMatch "\.(md|markdown)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Developer tooling. Python validators, and the test harnesses.
<FilesMatch "\.(py|mjs|sh|bat|ps1)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Build and dependency manifests — they enumerate versions, which is a
# vulnerability shopping list.
<FilesMatch "^(composer\.(json|lock)|package(-lock)?\.json|artisan|\.env.*|.*\.log)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Anything beginning with a dot: .env, .git, .gitignore, .htaccess itself.
<FilesMatch "^\.">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# A vault export is a real secret in a file. It should never be in the web root
# at all; this is the second line of defence for when one gets left there.
<FilesMatch "\.(hisab-vault|sql|sqlite|db|bak|old|orig|swp|dist)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # The Laravel application layer. Every .php file under a module's backend/
  # directory is a controller, a model or a migration, and none of them is an
  # entry point — Laravel routes through public/index.php. Reaching one directly
  # executes it out of context.
  RewriteRule ^modules/[^/]+/backend/.*\.php$ - [F,L]

  # The framework's own directories, for the same reason.
  RewriteRule ^(app|bootstrap|config|database|storage|vendor|routes|tests)/ - [F,L]

  # Developer directories in full, not only by file extension: tools/ holds
  # HTML harnesses that frame the app, and docs/ holds the deployment notes.
  RewriteRule ^(tools|docs|research)/ - [F,L]

  # The .git directory. Blocked by the dotfile rule above as well; this catches
  # the case where a server is configured to allow dotfiles.
  RewriteRule ^\.git - [F,L]
</IfModule>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3 · MIME types
#
# A woff2 served as application/octet-stream still downloads and still renders,
# so this looks like it does not matter. It costs the compression and the
# correct caching, and a .webmanifest served as text/plain is rejected outright,
# which silently disables installing the app to a phone's home screen.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
  AddType font/woff2                    .woff2
  AddType image/svg+xml                 .svg
  AddType application/manifest+json     .webmanifest
  AddType text/javascript               .js .mjs
  AddType application/json              .json
  AddType text/css                      .css

  AddDefaultCharset UTF-8
  AddCharset UTF-8 .html .css .js .json .svg .webmanifest
</IfModule>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4 · Compression
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/css text/plain text/xml
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/javascript application/javascript
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/json application/manifest+json
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE image/svg+xml
  # woff2 is already compressed. Running deflate over it costs CPU on every
  # request and makes the file very slightly larger.
</IfModule>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5 · Caching
#
# THIS PROJECT HAS NO BUILD STEP, SO FILENAMES CARRY NO CONTENT HASH.
# That single fact decides every value below. A year-long cache on a stylesheet
# whose name never changes means a deploy reaches nobody who has visited before,
# and there is no way to force it. So:
#
#   · HTML          never cached. It is the document that points at everything.
#   · CSS and JS    one hour, and must-revalidate. Long enough to help a session,
#                   short enough that a fix lands the same day.
#   · fonts, icons  one year, immutable. Their content genuinely does not change;
#                   a new icon is a new symbol inside the same sprite, which is
#                   why the sprite is grouped with CSS below rather than here.
#   · seed JSON     one day. Read once per install.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>

  <FilesMatch "\.(css|js)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>

  # The sprite changes whenever an icon is added, and a stale one means missing
  # glyphs rather than an old colour, so it is treated as code.
  <FilesMatch "sprite\.svg$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>

  <FilesMatch "\.woff2$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  </FilesMatch>

  <FilesMatch "\.(png|jpg|jpeg|webp|avif|ico)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  </FilesMatch>

  <FilesMatch "\.json$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=86400"
  </FilesMatch>

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # 6 · Security headers
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  # No sniffing. A JSON response reinterpreted as HTML is the mechanism behind
  # a whole class of injection.
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"

  # A private ledger must never render inside someone else's page. DENY rather
  # than SAMEORIGIN: nothing in this app frames itself.
  Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"

  # Do not leak which page of a private app was open when following a link out.
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

  # Features this app does not use, switched off so a compromised script cannot
  # reach them. Notably the camera and the microphone: the vault has no scanner.
  Header always set Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), magnetometer=(), accelerometer=(), gyroscope=()"

  # Cross-origin isolation. Also what a future WASM Argon2 build would need.
  Header always set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
  Header always set Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy "same-origin"

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Content-Security-Policy
  #
  # The inline hash below is the theme block that runs before the first paint in
  # every page. It is NOT 'unsafe-inline': allowing inline script wholesale on a
  # page that decrypts secrets would leave the policy decorative, and this
  # product has exactly one inline script, identical in every page.
  #
  # REGENERATE IT AFTER ANY EDIT TO THAT BLOCK:
  #     python tools/check-pages.py
  # The tool fails the build if this string and the pages disagree.
  #
  # Everything else is 'self' because everything else IS self — no CDN, no
  # analytics, no webfont host. That is a deliberate choice for an app holding a
  # private ledger, and it is what makes a policy this tight possible.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-0qYJf+ZXLkvwd2kLmQlxzgzOLGnlT6zY7rLoYDOX09s='; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests"

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # HSTS — commented out ON PURPOSE.
  #
  # Enable it only once HTTPS is confirmed working on the real domain, including
  # every subdomain you use. It tells browsers to refuse plain http for two
  # years, and that instruction cannot be withdrawn from a browser that has
  # already received it. Turning it on before the certificate is right locks
  # people out of the site with no way to bypass.
  #
  # Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains"
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
</IfModule>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7 · Errors
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ErrorDocument 404 /404.html
ErrorDocument 403 /404.html

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8 · The Laravel API — NOT YET ACTIVE
#
# Uncomment when the backend exists. It routes /api/* into Laravel's front
# controller while leaving every static file served directly by the web server.
#
# The front controller lives in public/, and public/ is NOT the document root
# here — Hostinger fixes the document root at public_html, and moving the whole
# framework above it is the alternative arrangement documented in
# docs/DEPLOY-HOSTINGER.md §7.
#
# <IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
#   RewriteEngine On
#   RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} ^/api
#   RewriteRule ^api/?(.*)$ public/index.php [L,QSA]
# </IfModule>
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
